WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.408 --> 00:00:16.835
In his chambers at Gloucester castle, the earl said to Edmund, "I do not like taking orders from

2
00:00:16.875 --> 00:00:18.356
Lear's daughters and their husbands.

3
00:00:19.597 --> 00:00:21.498
But they are our rulers now.

4
00:00:21.958 --> 00:00:23.619
They tell me I must not help the king."

5
00:00:24.739 --> 00:00:25.420
"How cruel!"

6
00:00:25.720 --> 00:00:27.781
exclaimed Edmund, pretending to be shocked.

7
00:00:27.801 --> 00:00:29.622
"But there is more.

8
00:00:30.142 --> 00:00:33.024
I have a letter from Cordelia, now the queen of France.

9
00:00:34.228 --> 00:00:38.512
She has learned of her father's troubles, and the French army is on its way to defend the

10
00:00:38.552 --> 00:00:41.294
king. Now I shall go look for him.

11
00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:44.717
If Cornwall asks for me, tell him I have gone to bed.

12
00:00:45.838 --> 00:00:47.559
Do not say a word to him of these matters."

13
00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.061
And Gloucester went out into the storm.

14
00:00:51.302 --> 00:00:53.064
Edmund could not believe his luck.

15
00:00:53.084 --> 00:00:56.847
"I shall tell Cornwall everything immediately," he said to himself.

16
00:00:58.028 --> 00:01:02.512
"When he hears that my father has disobeyed him, Cornwall will surely reward me."

17
00:01:04.072 --> 00:01:08.355
Meanwhile, Lear was wandering on the heath, accompanied by the faithful Kent.

18
00:01:08.375 --> 00:01:10.757
"Blow winds, blow!"

19
00:01:11.297 --> 00:01:11.798
roared Lear.

20
00:01:11.818 --> 00:01:14.099
"Lightning, burn my white head!

21
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:16.801
You will still be kinder than my daughters!"

22
00:01:18.082 --> 00:01:21.825
A few days earlier, Kent had also received a letter from Cordelia.

23
00:01:21.845 --> 00:01:25.327
"I am coming to England with an army," she wrote.

24
00:01:25.707 --> 00:01:27.269
"We shall land at Dover."

25
00:01:27.289 --> 00:01:31.932
"If we can get the king to Dover," Kent thought, "he might be safe."

26
00:01:33.088 --> 00:01:36.069
"My lord," he said to Lear, "we must get away from here."

27
00:01:37.149 --> 00:01:38.810
But Lear continued to rage.

28
00:01:38.830 --> 00:01:41.630
"The gods are punishing the wicked with this storm.

29
00:01:42.151 --> 00:01:44.991
But I am a man more sinned against than sinning."

30
00:01:45.011 --> 00:01:48.512
"I see an old shack in the distance," said Kent.

31
00:01:48.973 --> 00:01:50.073
"It will give you some shelter."

32
00:01:51.513 --> 00:01:52.574
Lear stared at Kent.

33
00:01:52.594 --> 00:01:56.014
"Good Caius, I fear I am losing my mind.

34
00:01:57.175 --> 00:02:01.056
This storm seems horrible to you, but to me, it seems like nothing.

35
00:02:02.124 --> 00:02:06.207
When one experiences greater suffering, one barely notices the lesser."

36
00:02:07.288 --> 00:02:10.690
When they got to the shack, Lear looked inside and then jumped back.

37
00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:13.192
"What is that strange creature?"

38
00:02:13.512 --> 00:02:14.292
he said fearfully.

39
00:02:14.793 --> 00:02:17.555
Kent peered in and saw a man curled up in the corner.

40
00:02:18.675 --> 00:02:22.158
He had mud on his face, and his hair was dirty and tangled.

41
00:02:22.618 --> 00:02:23.879
He was muttering nonsense.

42
00:02:23.899 --> 00:02:25.600
"Who are you?"

43
00:02:26.020 --> 00:02:26.501
said Kent.

44
00:02:27.541 --> 00:02:28.382
The man stood up.

45
00:02:28.402 --> 00:02:31.364
"I am Poor Tom," he cackled.

46
00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:37.201
"Wicked spirits have chased me through fire and flame and put poison in my porridge.

47
00:02:37.781 --> 00:02:39.062
I beg of you some charity."

48
00:02:39.562 --> 00:02:40.582
He held out his hand.

49
00:02:40.602 --> 00:02:45.624
"He's just a harmless madman and a beggar," said Kent to Lear.

50
00:02:47.084 --> 00:02:51.705
Neither man realized that Poor Tom was actually Edgar, Gloucester's son.

51
00:02:52.906 --> 00:02:56.967
Knowing his father wanted him dead, he was hiding in the countryside in disguise.

52
00:02:56.987 --> 00:03:00.728
"Did his cruel daughters make him crazy?"

53
00:03:01.188 --> 00:03:01.788
Lear ranted.

54
00:03:02.912 --> 00:03:06.113
"Why, he has only a thin blanket to protect him from the storm.

55
00:03:07.293 --> 00:03:11.814
Is man no more than this: a poor, bare, pitiful animal?

56
00:03:12.355 --> 00:03:14.255
Then I too am just like this beggar!"

57
00:03:15.455 --> 00:03:19.677
Lear began to tear off his own clothes as Kent tried to calm him.

58
00:03:19.697 --> 00:03:22.077
"Beware of the evil spirit!

59
00:03:22.557 --> 00:03:23.598
Obey your parents!

60
00:03:24.218 --> 00:03:25.378
Poor Tom is cold.

61
00:03:25.598 --> 00:03:28.659
Look!" cried Edgar, "here comes a walking fire."

62
00:03:29.819 --> 00:03:31.680
Gloucester appeared, carrying a torch.

63
00:03:32.232 --> 00:03:34.933
He did not recognize Edgar, his own son, either.

64
00:03:34.953 --> 00:03:40.695
"My lord," the earl said to Lear, "your daughters and the Duke of Cornwall have forbidden me to

65
00:03:40.755 --> 00:03:41.135
help you.

66
00:03:42.195 --> 00:03:44.496
But I cannot obey such heartless orders.

67
00:03:45.056 --> 00:03:46.656
Let me take you to a warmer shelter."

68
00:03:46.676 --> 00:03:51.198
"I must speak more with Poor Tom," muttered Lear.

69
00:03:51.218 --> 00:03:52.578
"He is very wise."

70
00:03:52.598 --> 00:03:58.440
"I fear his grief has made him lose his mind," whispered Kent to Gloucester.

71
00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:02.182
"I understand his grief too well," replied the earl.

72
00:04:02.202 --> 00:04:06.605
"I have a son I loved dearly, who also has betrayed me."

73
00:04:07.105 --> 00:04:07.966
He turned to Lear.

74
00:04:07.986 --> 00:04:09.086
"Come, my lord."

75
00:04:10.127 --> 00:04:13.469
Gloucester led Lear, Kent, and Edgar to a farmhouse.

76
00:04:14.070 --> 00:04:16.651
Then he left to find some food for the king.

77
00:04:17.792 --> 00:04:21.395
By now, Lear was acting as crazy as Edgar pretended to be.

78
00:04:21.415 --> 00:04:26.878
"Look," he babbled, pointing at two stools, "it's Goneril and Regan!

79
00:04:28.059 --> 00:04:31.686
We will put them on trial, and Poor Tom will be the judge!

80
00:04:32.226 --> 00:04:34.128
But first, I must rest."

81
00:04:34.749 --> 00:04:37.191
Exhausted, he lay down and fell asleep.

82
00:04:38.332 --> 00:04:40.334
Gloucester soon returned, very upset.

83
00:04:40.354 --> 00:04:43.797
"I just heard about a plot to kill the king," he told Kent.

84
00:04:43.817 --> 00:04:47.441
"You must take him to Dover, where he will be safe.

85
00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:50.183
Quick—I have a stretcher waiting outside.

86
00:04:50.684 --> 00:04:52.205
Lay the king on it and go now!"

87
00:04:53.687 --> 00:04:59.281
Back at Gloucester Castle, Edmund approached Cornwall and told him about the letter his father had received from

88
00:04:59.321 --> 00:05:05.083
Cordelia. "My lord," said Edmund, "it grieves me that my own father is a traitor.

89
00:05:05.603 --> 00:05:08.344
He supports Lear and Cordelia instead of you."

90
00:05:09.524 --> 00:05:10.924
Cornwall's eyes narrowed.

91
00:05:10.944 --> 00:05:12.365
"You have done well.

92
00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:16.146
I appoint you Earl of Gloucester in place of your father.

93
00:05:16.646 --> 00:05:18.466
He must be caught and punished."

94
00:05:19.667 --> 00:05:21.867
Cornwall then summoned his wife and Goneril.

95
00:05:22.908 --> 00:05:26.321
To Goneril, he said, "Spies say the French have landed.

96
00:05:27.521 --> 00:05:31.042
Ride to your castle and tell your husband to prepare for war.

97
00:05:31.582 --> 00:05:33.162
And seek out the traitor Gloucester."

98
00:05:33.182 --> 00:05:36.343
"He should hang," Regan declared.

99
00:05:36.363 --> 00:05:39.164
"Leave him to me," said Cornwall.

100
00:05:39.684 --> 00:05:41.205
"Edmund, accompany Goneril.

101
00:05:42.305 --> 00:05:45.586
You do not want to see what I shall do to your father when he is found."

102
00:05:47.086 --> 00:05:51.087
Shortly after Edmund and Goneril's departure, Gloucester returned to the castle.

103
00:05:51.107 --> 00:05:54.937
"Why, it's the traitor," Regan snarled.

104
00:05:54.957 --> 00:05:57.438
"Seize him," she ordered the servants.

105
00:05:58.579 --> 00:05:59.540
"What do you mean by this?"

106
00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:01.221
asked Gloucester, trembling.

107
00:06:01.241 --> 00:06:02.562
"You are my guests!"

108
00:06:03.783 --> 00:06:05.965
"Tie him to a chair," Cornwall commanded.

109
00:06:05.985 --> 00:06:08.046
"Where have you sent the king?"

110
00:06:08.566 --> 00:06:09.247
demanded Regan.

111
00:06:09.267 --> 00:06:13.790
"To Dover, to escape you and your sister," said Gloucester.

112
00:06:13.810 --> 00:06:16.272
"I will see the king in his rightful place again!"

113
00:06:16.292 --> 00:06:20.275
"You shall see nothing," hissed Cornwall.

114
00:06:20.735 --> 00:06:24.290
He leaned forward and gouged out Gloucester's right eye.

115
00:06:24.810 --> 00:06:26.371
Gloucester howled in pain.

116
00:06:27.592 --> 00:06:33.556
But when Cornwall moved to gouge out the left eye, a servant loyal to Gloucester shouted, "Stop, my

117
00:06:33.616 --> 00:06:35.297
lord!" He was waving a sword.

118
00:06:35.317 --> 00:06:37.498
"How dare you!"

119
00:06:37.899 --> 00:06:38.659
shouted Cornwall.

120
00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:40.761
He drew his sword, and they fought.

121
00:06:41.841 --> 00:06:46.344
The servant managed to strike Cornwall once, hard, before Regan grabbed a knife.

122
00:06:46.965 --> 00:06:49.627
Running up behind the servant, she stabbed him dead.

123
00:06:49.647 --> 00:06:52.484
"And now the other one!"

124
00:06:52.964 --> 00:06:54.885
Cornwall said, turning back to Gloucester.

125
00:06:55.385 --> 00:06:57.286
And he put out Gloucester's left eye.

126
00:06:58.466 --> 00:06:59.946
"Help! Where is Edmund?"

127
00:07:00.466 --> 00:07:01.187
groaned Gloucester.

128
00:07:01.207 --> 00:07:05.648
"You are calling for the wrong son," Regan told the old man.

129
00:07:05.668 --> 00:07:08.669
"It was Edmund who told us you betrayed us!"

130
00:07:10.009 --> 00:07:13.010
In a flash, Gloucester realized Edmund's plot.

131
00:07:13.030 --> 00:07:16.031
"Then I have done Edgar wrong," he sobbed.

132
00:07:16.051 --> 00:07:17.851
"May he forgive me!"

133
00:07:17.871 --> 00:07:21.012
"Throw him out," said Regan to the servants.

134
00:07:21.504 --> 00:07:23.345
"Let him smell his way to Dover."

135
00:07:23.365 --> 00:07:27.147
"Come, Regan, help me," moaned Cornwall.

136
00:07:27.167 --> 00:07:28.128
"I'm bleeding."

137
00:07:29.628 --> 00:07:33.070
Gloucester was thrown out of the castle and began to stumble down the road.

138
00:07:34.151 --> 00:07:37.893
He didn't get far before Edgar, still disguised, saw him.

139
00:07:37.913 --> 00:07:41.035
"My poor father," he thought, horrified.

140
00:07:41.055 --> 00:07:42.856
"Is someone there?"

141
00:07:43.316 --> 00:07:44.096
called out Gloucester.

142
00:07:44.116 --> 00:07:47.358
"It's Poor Tom," said Edgar.

143
00:07:47.378 --> 00:07:49.139
"The mad beggar?"

144
00:07:49.159 --> 00:07:50.960
"Yes, my lord.

145
00:07:51.404 --> 00:07:51.884
Bless you."

146
00:07:51.904 --> 00:07:54.426
"Do you know the road to Dover?"

147
00:07:55.006 --> 00:07:55.647
asked the earl.

148
00:07:55.667 --> 00:07:57.548
"I will pay you to take me there."

149
00:07:57.568 --> 00:08:00.369
"Here's my arm," said Edgar.

150
00:08:00.389 --> 00:08:02.110
"Poor Tom will lead you."

151
00:08:03.271 --> 00:08:09.395
Leaning on Edgar's arm, Gloucester said, "These are strange times, when madmen lead the blind."

152
00:08:09.915 --> 00:08:11.356
Together, they set off.
